EC2 User Data – Automate web server deployment
User Data allows you to execute a script automatically when launching an EC2 instance.

Example: Amazon Linux User Data script
#!/bin/bash

# Update packages
yum update -y

# Install Apache
yum install httpd -y

# Start and enable Apache
systemctl start httpd
systemctl enable httpd

# Create website
cat > /var/www/html/index.html <<'EOF'
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Automated AWS Website</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome!</h1>
    <p>This website was deployed automatically using EC2 User Data.</p>
</body>
</html>
EOF


Example: User Data script using GitHub
#!/bin/bash

yum update -y
yum install httpd git -y

systemctl start httpd
systemctl enable httpd

cd /tmp
git clone <repository-url>

cp -r <repository-name>/* /var/www/html/


Note: Replace the repository URL and repository name with your actual GitHub details.
Important concepts to remember
- yum: Package manager used in Amazon Linux.
- systemctl: Manages Linux services.
- sudo: Executes commands with elevated privileges.
- mkdir: Creates directories.
- cat: Creates or writes file contents.
- nano: Opens a text editor in the terminal.
- git clone: Downloads a repository from GitHub.
- /var/www/html: Apache's default document root on Amazon Linux.
- #!/bin/bash: Specifies Bash as the script interpreter.
One important AWS configuration: Your EC2 Security Group must allow inbound HTTP traffic on port 80 for users to access your hosted website through the public IP address.
